[[:db/retract 17592186124916 :question/answer-fields 17592186122096] {:db/id 17592186124491 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c8443228-62d0-53de-820f-4c2c2789eb2f" "question-30d93e6c-378b-5de5-9291-694547fd0977"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124304 :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the vectors in $\\mathcal{C}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ are\n![](images/da/daf4111656a525abf51bf58105831ed11378ed7fc52915e1e58ca5f75884bcf0.svg)\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $\\mathcal{C}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n![](images/be/bed6471ce9aed8eb16a0d35f1d9985b8e99941f7c1326645de971960bdb5aedd.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124492 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b28dc41f-f44a-5439-b028-42214a679df4" "question-d6ffc405-f81c-5336-ad94-25ea55190ac1" "question-9a2d831b-a3f1-5313-8aee-60f8e3a93edc"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124305 :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2,\\mathbf{b}_3\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and let $\\mathcal{S}$ be the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ Find the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ if \n\n![](images/95/957c59d27a871a52f110c3dca91cec390091c13ceba6e14d6485f92aa150c51a.svg)\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is \n\n![](images/60/6095f1a65a9b9b245de2dd4cad611d2616a2b3b15e4701fe33c3c53b913c2283.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Recall  that the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ consists of the vectors $\\mathbf e_1, \\mathbf e_2,$ and $\\mathbf e_3,$ given by\n![](images/41/41bd0d4956d8ec09d0339a79329c6739e07e698d9dee9f192968d77c9ec28842.svg)\n\nThe components of the vectors in $\\mathcal{B}$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n![](images/30/30a1bdc2316f5d7acc527cb1d69fc11c7b732f9e22a4d57a11ed84f2db1e3cd5.svg)\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{S}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n![](images/56/5606e00ccc39829e3c599aa86286dcc1f1cb4e3febb280adf4e1bbe18dc7ab6a.svg)\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=0,$ and $c=10.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124493 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-df33f267-a622-55d4-b6ca-07fa624cf0e9" "question-f1424ed2-0052-5fff-908d-b6551bfbb11a" "question-a26dc326-54d9-5ae2-96c3-423118f36c64"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124306 :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by \n![](images/46/4676de86bd5b4bc2a8e1d6a9432379d0769163113913137f8143a71eb16e8240.svg)\nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124494 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-699ab275-8547-554b-97d2-1ddcd2846071" "question-4f239f11-8e8b-53a2-a96f-22cc233efad1" "question-d9fbd56e-ebad-5e95-b783-252de92d505c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124307 :question/problem "Let ![](images/07/076634439afec4c0e4fb0a443d88163245269988e323ddb9d83d1a4a1b18139e.svg) and ![](images/b2/b2fd30034f45fb2d8b06f2697f137c93de21942822b08193c3e640ef51d4e7c0.svg) be bases of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\},$ respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$\n\nSo, we need to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n![](images/dd/dd733ebebc7131eb2762e6e98e1d14d07a730e5e37657c639ae9cc46be123ee2.svg)\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 7 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 13\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 & 22 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}= & [\\begin{aligned}\\,4 & 22 \\\\ \\,1 & 13\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-c8443228-62d0-53de-820f-4c2c2789eb2f" :question/id #uuid "c8443228-62d0-53de-820f-4c2c2789eb2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-113104" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}.$ \nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ from $B$ to $C$ given that\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{2}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ddc74432-9277-50d2-8b8f-c46b82fbfa4b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ddc74432-9277-50d2-8b8f-c46b82fbfa4b" :answer-field/id #uuid "ddc74432-9277-50d2-8b8f-c46b82fbfa4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-02b7ac1d-4efd-51c0-b67d-14536d1d6f75" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8d8542ed-fbae-5713-bdec-3842f4a51f81" "answer-59e9f366-67f4-5425-be8d-c3bdec30dc1a" "answer-f19d556d-427e-5f81-8e9f-38999a23661f" "answer-e96a7aec-7264-57b6-b1e0-4a5a84fb5122" "answer-02b7ac1d-4efd-51c0-b67d-14536d1d6f75"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d8542ed-fbae-5713-bdec-3842f4a51f81" :answer/id #uuid "8d8542ed-fbae-5713-bdec-3842f4a51f81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-59e9f366-67f4-5425-be8d-c3bdec30dc1a" :answer/id #uuid "59e9f366-67f4-5425-be8d-c3bdec30dc1a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f19d556d-427e-5f81-8e9f-38999a23661f" :answer/id #uuid "f19d556d-427e-5f81-8e9f-38999a23661f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e96a7aec-7264-57b6-b1e0-4a5a84fb5122" :answer/id #uuid "e96a7aec-7264-57b6-b1e0-4a5a84fb5122" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-02b7ac1d-4efd-51c0-b67d-14536d1d6f75" :answer/id #uuid "02b7ac1d-4efd-51c0-b67d-14536d1d6f75" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-30d93e6c-378b-5de5-9291-694547fd0977" :question/id #uuid "30d93e6c-378b-5de5-9291-694547fd0977" :question/math-academy-id "q-36354" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ be bases of ${R}^{3}.$ \nWhat are the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & =2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}, \\\\ {b}_{2} & =4{c}_{1}-2{c}_{3}, \\\\ {b}_{3} & =2{c}_{1}-3{c}_{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & a & 2 \\\\ b & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & c & -3\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the vectors in $B$ relative to the basis $C$ are\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{2}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{3}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=4,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4928869d-0a7f-5e17-a81c-6aaf1def0a0c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4928869d-0a7f-5e17-a81c-6aaf1def0a0c" :answer-field/id #uuid "4928869d-0a7f-5e17-a81c-6aaf1def0a0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2344242d-6b96-5993-b8b0-de107b4279a2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b19c2423-673d-5c2f-9566-5866776b9ddd" "answer-54569e6c-7221-5713-add5-c98abf257a21" "answer-a8c37f7a-6b0a-5951-912a-c0791c4bd8ac" "answer-2344242d-6b96-5993-b8b0-de107b4279a2" "answer-3879c262-51c9-5503-937f-270c4a66ac2c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b19c2423-673d-5c2f-9566-5866776b9ddd" :answer/id #uuid "b19c2423-673d-5c2f-9566-5866776b9ddd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=4,$ and $c=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54569e6c-7221-5713-add5-c98abf257a21" :answer/id #uuid "54569e6c-7221-5713-add5-c98abf257a21" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=4,$ and $c=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a8c37f7a-6b0a-5951-912a-c0791c4bd8ac" :answer/id #uuid "a8c37f7a-6b0a-5951-912a-c0791c4bd8ac" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=0,$ and $c=4$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2344242d-6b96-5993-b8b0-de107b4279a2" :answer/id #uuid "2344242d-6b96-5993-b8b0-de107b4279a2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=4,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3879c262-51c9-5503-937f-270c4a66ac2c" :answer/id #uuid "3879c262-51c9-5503-937f-270c4a66ac2c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=4,$ $b=1,$ and $c=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b28dc41f-f44a-5439-b028-42214a679df4" :question/id #uuid "b28dc41f-f44a-5439-b028-42214a679df4" :question/math-academy-id "q-36402" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ \nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to S}$ from $B$ to $S$ if \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall  that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2},$ given by\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe components of the vectors of $B$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{ and }\\,[{b}_{2}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $S.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-987253ee-4461-5046-b1db-ce4100ac59cc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-987253ee-4461-5046-b1db-ce4100ac59cc" :answer-field/id #uuid "987253ee-4461-50