{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall  that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2},$ given by\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe components of the vectors of $B$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{2}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $S.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0c86502d-b8b8-5473-bf1a-5d435069f0e5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0c86502d-b8b8-5473-bf1a-5d435069f0e5" :answer-field/id #uuid "0c86502d-b8b8-5473-bf1a-5d435069f0e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-16853ab7-760d-5058-8e30-4207c809785e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-16853ab7-760d-5058-8e30-4207c809785e" "answer-87e78e9b-fea5-57d2-9ca3-96814468f5f9" "answer-c80a8828-4df2-559c-b9ff-6f138f31421c" "answer-b9b03fb3-ebd1-50f3-8cee-c5c60249d1de" "answer-07838b3a-5d21-5d4b-96ed-ad6dca8941c5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16853ab7-760d-5058-8e30-4207c809785e" :answer/id #uuid "16853ab7-760d-5058-8e30-4207c809785e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87e78e9b-fea5-57d2-9ca3-96814468f5f9" :answer/id #uuid "87e78e9b-fea5-57d2-9ca3-96814468f5f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c80a8828-4df2-559c-b9ff-6f138f31421c" :answer/id #uuid "c80a8828-4df2-559c-b9ff-6f138f31421c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b9b03fb3-ebd1-50f3-8cee-c5c60249d1de" :answer/id #uuid "b9b03fb3-ebd1-50f3-8cee-c5c60249d1de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07838b3a-5d21-5d4b-96ed-ad6dca8941c5" :answer/id #uuid "07838b3a-5d21-5d4b-96ed-ad6dca8941c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9a2d831b-a3f1-5313-8aee-60f8e3a93edc" :question/id #uuid "9a2d831b-a3f1-5313-8aee-60f8e3a93edc" :question/math-academy-id "q-81852" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What are the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ if \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & a & 7 \\\\ -1 & 4 & b \\\\ 4 & c & -9\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall  that the standard basis of ${R}^{3}$ consists of the vectors ${e}_{1},{e}_{2},$ and ${e}_{3},$ given by\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe components of the vectors in $B$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{2}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{3}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $S.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7 & -9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=8,$ $b=5,$ and $c=7.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8192819f-c781-5fbc-98ce-44030ec1bb27"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8192819f-c781-5fbc-98ce-44030ec1bb27" :answer-field/id #uuid "8192819f-c781-5fbc-98ce-44030ec1bb27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6c73720e-ba56-5223-a2a5-3a7a43ab5d38" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7a0f28c3-3134-5225-add6-826343463b74" "answer-26ea652b-6e02-523c-b2ba-b95e1959da46" "answer-6615ba74-1fb7-56ad-903d-4b77c16b3dd6" "answer-dcc6f90c-1011-5415-bdbb-61b071aed3be" "answer-6c73720e-ba56-5223-a2a5-3a7a43ab5d38"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a0f28c3-3134-5225-add6-826343463b74" :answer/id #uuid "7a0f28c3-3134-5225-add6-826343463b74" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=7,$ and $c=5.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26ea652b-6e02-523c-b2ba-b95e1959da46" :answer/id #uuid "26ea652b-6e02-523c-b2ba-b95e1959da46" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=8,$ and $c=5.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6615ba74-1fb7-56ad-903d-4b77c16b3dd6" :answer/id #uuid "6615ba74-1fb7-56ad-903d-4b77c16b3dd6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=7,$ $b=5,$ and $c=8.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dcc6f90c-1011-5415-bdbb-61b071aed3be" :answer/id #uuid "dcc6f90c-1011-5415-bdbb-61b071aed3be" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=8,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=7.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c73720e-ba56-5223-a2a5-3a7a43ab5d38" :answer/id #uuid "6c73720e-ba56-5223-a2a5-3a7a43ab5d38" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=8,$ $b=5,$ and $c=7.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-df33f267-a622-55d4-b6ca-07fa624cf0e9" :question/id #uuid "df33f267-a622-55d4-b6ca-07fa624cf0e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-113410" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $C$ to the basis $B$ is given by\n${P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$  ." :question/worked-solution "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of ${P}_{C\\to B}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C} & ={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 4-5\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2cc46cdd-beb2-5ee4-a2a8-1259b1fb90ca"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2cc46cdd-beb2-5ee4-a2a8-1259b1fb90ca" :answer-field/id #uuid "2cc46cdd-beb2-5ee4-a2a8-1259b1fb90ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7fde4386-2338-52c7-a292-c73f0e034d10" :answer-field/choices ["answer-71d81bba-103c-52d1-9ba7-ba524273847c" "answer-7fde4386-2338-52c7-a292-c73f0e034d10" "answer-9cf2c82b-0f69-5967-94d6-03180ca54721" "answer-60bc38dd-e04b-53b1-91b2-6bb378d41837" "answer-ca7a94f0-7d2a-56ba-bb59-168b33d3bb36"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71d81bba-103c-52d1-9ba7-ba524273847c" :answer/id #uuid "71d81bba-103c-52d1-9ba7-ba524273847c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 5 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7fde4386-2338-52c7-a292-c73f0e034d10" :answer/id #uuid "7fde4386-2338-52c7-a292-c73f0e034d10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9cf2c82b-0f69-5967-94d6-03180ca54721" :answer/id #uuid "9cf2c82b-0f69-5967-94d6-03180ca54721" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60bc38dd-e04b-53b1-91b2-6bb378d41837" :answer/id #uuid "60bc38dd-e04b-53b1-91b2-6bb378d41837" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca7a94f0-7d2a-56ba-bb59-168b33d3bb36" :answer/id #uuid "ca7a94f0-7d2a-56ba-bb59-168b33d3bb36" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f1424ed2-0052-5fff-908d-b6551bfbb11a" :question/id #uuid "f1424ed2-0052-5fff-908d-b6551bfbb11a" :question/math-academy-id "q-81857" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $C$ to the basis $B$ is given by\n${P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$  ." :question/worked-solution "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of ${P}_{C\\to B}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C} & ={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-6)\\cdot 4-(-5)\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-74971671-dff2-5641-9515-43efb29ddf1c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-74971671-dff2-5641-9515-43efb29ddf1c" :answer-field/id #uuid "74971671-dff2-5641-9515-43efb29ddf1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6c8d2ddf-0d76-50dd-b764-f107e2c723e8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6fb38a3d-724f-59ef-90ac-504aa9672e91" "answer-6a9c0465-d9d9-5b77-b798-d3d852db486e" "answer-6c8d2ddf-0d76-50dd-b764-f107e2c723e8" "answer-172c82bc-392e-5ec7-9deb-ddba9ffa9f17" "answer-e2f776f7-8d29-51a7-9c76-2fe228c44b24"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6fb38a3d-724f-59ef-90ac-504aa9672e91" :answer/id #uuid "6fb38a3d-724f-59ef-90ac-504aa9672e91" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a9c0465-d9d9-5b77-b798-d3d852db486e" :answer/id #uuid "6a9c0465-d9d9-5b77-b798-d3d852db486e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c8d2ddf-0d76-50dd-b764-f107e2c723e8" :answer/id #uuid "6c8d2ddf-0d76-50dd-b764-f107e2c723e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-172c82bc-392e-5ec7-9deb-ddba9ffa9f17" :answer/id #uuid "172c82bc-392e-5ec7-9deb-ddba9ffa9f17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e2f776f7-8d29-51a7-9c76-2fe228c44b24" :answer/id #uuid "e2f776f7-8d29-51a7-9c76-2fe228c44b24" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a26dc326-54d9-5ae2-96c3-423118f36c64" :question/id #uuid "a26dc326-54d9-5ae2-96c3-423118f36c64" :question/math-academy-id "q-113409" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ is given by \n${P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$  ." :question/worked-solution "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-04d718c2-a214-5ef1-82b5-17f86c63db92"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-04d718c2-a214-5ef1-82b5-17f86c63db92" :answer-field/id #uuid "04d718c2-a214-5ef1-82b5-17f86c63db92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-90c78f39-cbdd-5735-93c9-853cf06b1825" :answer-field/choices ["answer-aa836202-b0e6-521c-8443-a083a03ee71c" "answer-3e6f0908-6c0d-5c24-87f5-821d61c33178" "answer-90c78f39-cbdd-5735-93c9-853cf06b1825" "answer-b249e81c-9b0f-550d-b583-627092bcf567" "answer-2a7deef8-19c9-5a99-beb9-30ea6eec4a82"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aa836202-b0e6-521c-8443-a083a03ee71c" :answer/id #uuid "aa836202-b0e6-521c-8443-a083a03ee71c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e6f0908-6c0d-5c24-87f5-821d61c33178" :answer/id #uuid "3e6f0908-6c0d-5c24-87f5-821d61c33178" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90c78f39-cbdd-5735-93c9-853cf06b1825" :answer/id #uuid "90c78f39-cbdd-5735-93c9-853cf06b1825" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b249e81c-9b0f-550d-b583-627092bcf567" :answer/id #uuid "b249e81c-9b0f-550d-b583-627092bcf567" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a7deef8-19c9-5a99-beb9-30ea6eec4a82" :answer/id #uuid "2a7deef8-19c9-5a99-beb9-30ea6eec4a82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & -1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-699ab275-8547-554b-97d2-1ddcd2846071" :question/id #uuid "699ab275-8547-554b-97d2-1ddcd2846071" :question/math-academy-id "q-113508" :question/problem "Let $B={[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}$ and $C={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5a77bdea-a6bc-5acf-8e6b-c3386884ffde"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5a77bdea-a6bc-5acf-8e6b-c3386884ffde" :answer-field/id #uuid "5a77bdea-a6bc-5acf-8e6b-c3386884ffde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f89b032a-28b2-5d9f-9d76-0bd18854f341" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b825f629-82ac-53f3-b87f-12a36ddac7c4" "answer-f89b032a-28b2-5d9f-9d76-0bd18854f341" "answer-8fca4fa8-84ec-5284-b191-c15918cd020b" "answer-caa59a06-8d44-5e97-8650-fe3fd7d19fda" "answer-40f45c93-6fe2-5f26-851c-9bd122ebd2a2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b825f629-82ac-53f3-b87f-12a36ddac7c4" :answer/id #uuid "b825f629-82ac-53f3-b87f-12a36ddac7c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f89b032a-28b2-5d9f-9d76-0bd18854f341" :answer/id #uuid "f89b032a-28b2-5d9f-9d76-0bd18854f341" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8fca4fa8-84ec-5284-b191-c15918cd020b" :answer/id #uuid "8fca4fa8-84ec-5284-b191-c15918cd020b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-caa59a06-8d44-5e97-8650-fe3fd7d19fda" :answer/id #uuid "caa59a06-8d44-5e97-8650-fe3fd7d19fda" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-40f45c93-6fe2-5f26-851c-9bd122ebd2a2" :answer/id #uuid "40f45c93-6fe2-5f26-851c-9bd122ebd2a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4f239f11-8e8b-53a2-a96f-22cc233efad1" :question/id #uuid "4f239f11-8e8b-53a2-a96f-22cc233efad1" :question/math-academy-id "q-113526" :question/problem "Let $B={[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}$  be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $S$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{e}_{1},{e}_{2}}$, respectively. Recall that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $S$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {e}_{1} & {e}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-6){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{S\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-08ec3541-b791-5354-9296-f775e0ff2635"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-08ec3541-b791-5354-9296-f775e0ff2635" :answer-field/id #uuid "08ec3541-b791-5354-9296-f775e0ff2635" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1100f1bc-ed34-5a67-be8b-4cd9cbd023d1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-50b04112-6a4b-5de0-8b36-cac7c5a0a40d" "answer-a2edcdc2-4bd9-58b5-8e0b-973a0e0009a0" "answer-945a35b8-1727-56f0-8d1f-a7a7ff35591a" "answer-1100f1bc-ed34-5a67-be8b-4cd9cbd023d1" "answer-5a88c79a-d8b2-5e43-8a47-5013ebd4e46e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50b04112-6a4b-5de0-8b36-cac7c5a0a40d" :answer/id #uuid "50b04112-6a4b-5de0-8b36-cac7c5a0a40d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2edcdc2-4bd9-58b5-8e0b-973a0e0009a0" :answer/id #uuid "a2edcdc2-4bd9-58b5-8e0b-973a0e0009a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-945a35b8-1727-56f0-8d1f-a7a7ff35591a" :answer/id #uuid "945a35b8-1727-56f0-8d1f-a7a7ff35591a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1100f1bc-ed34-5a67-be8b-4cd9cbd023d1" :answer/id #uuid "1100f1bc-ed34-5a67-be8b-4cd9cbd023d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5a88c79a-d8b2-5e43-8a47-5013ebd4e46e" :answer/id #uuid "5a88c79a-d8b2-5e43-8a47-5013ebd4e46e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d9fbd56e-ebad-5e95-b783-252de92d505c" :question/id #uuid "d9fbd56e-ebad-5e95-b783-252de92d505c" :question/math-academy-id "q-36358" :question/problem "Let $B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}$  be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $S$ is the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $S$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{e}_{1},{e}_{2}}$, respectively. Recall that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $S$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {e}_{1} & {e}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -6 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{S\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dbb6c09b-dbdd-53f8-b483-27b433b6c2c3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dbb6c09b-dbdd-53f8-b483-27b433b6c2c3" :answer-field/id #uuid "dbb6c09b-dbdd-53f8-b483-27b433b6c2c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c0f96822-b423-5c09-9a06-dd548ce5f549" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3b26590d-792e-51f9-b6ee-9b92d34732ca" "answer-57bd0aaf-7e37-5d1f-aa28-5056aba60ec5" "answer-419a7dcc-0ec9-5201-aaac-a0d26fd4a862" "answer-3c3ac9c4-89a8-53e0-9f5b-0281ad0e261f" "answer-c0f96822-b423-5c09-9a06-dd548ce5f549"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b26590d-792e-51f9-b6ee-9b92d34732ca" :answer/id #uuid "3b26590d-792e-51f9-b6ee-9b92d34732ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-57bd0aaf-7e37-5d1f-aa28-5056aba60ec5" :answer/id #uuid "57bd0aaf-7e37-5d1f-aa28-5056aba60ec5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-419a7dcc-0ec9-5201-aaac-a0d26fd4a862" :answer/id #uuid "419a7dcc-0ec9-5201-aaac-a0d26fd4a862" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c3ac9c4-89a8-53e0-9f5b-0281ad0e261f" :answer/id #uuid "3c3ac9c4-89a8-53e0-9f5b-0281ad0e261f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c0f96822-b423-5c09-9a06-dd548ce5f549" :answer/id #uuid "c0f96822-b423-5c09-9a06-dd548ce5f549" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124916 :question/problem "Let $B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}$  be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $S$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{e}_{1},{e}_{2}}$, respectively. Recall that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $S$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {e}_{1} & {e}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{S\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-249ce60b-00ae-5912-900c-fca2a8f69bfe"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-249ce60b-00ae-5912-900c-fca2a8f69bfe" :answer-field/id #uuid "249ce60b-00ae-5912-900c-fca2a8f69bfe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4dd79918-775f-5123-b336-42017be04695" :answer-field/choices ["answer-20235027-ad55-59fe-b1e5-b1705f888d17" "answer-7c7fa779-b799-54a8-ad4c-a9e9dcea93d0" "answer-4dd79918-775f-5123-b336-42017be04695" "answer-7928c32a-4f5a-5fbd-ab9a-645a98853355" "answer-ead71fbb-62b9-559f-94fe-b5cd728b52ed"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20235027-ad55-59fe-b1e5-b1705f888d17" :answer/id #uuid "20235027-ad55-59fe-b1e5-b1705f888d17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c7fa779-b799-54a8-ad4c-a9e9dcea93d0" :answer/id #uuid "7c7fa779-b799-54a8-ad4c-a9e9dcea93d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4dd79918-775f-5123-b336-42017be04695" :answer/id #uuid "4dd79918-775f-5123-b336-42017be04695" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7928c32a-4f5a-5fbd-ab9a-645a98853355" :answer/id #uuid "7928c32a-4f5a-5fbd-ab9a-645a98853355" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ead71fbb-62b9-559f-94fe-b5cd728b52ed" :answer/id #uuid "ead71fbb-62b9-559f-94fe-b5cd728b52ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125766 :tutorial/content "Given two bases $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$ of a vector space $V,$ the **change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$** is the matrix $P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}},$ whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors from $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$.\n\nIn expanded form, the matrix looks as follows:\n![](images/06/062187705e6074afa6b5356be64f8deb878be540e2c3b236b22dbd244b872cc6.svg)\n\nFor example, let's consider two bases $\\mathcal B$ and $\\mathcal C$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ given by \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2\\}, \\qquad\\mathcal{C}=\\{\\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2\\}.\n$$\n\nIf we happen to know that\n\n![](images/32/32409a45b5ebcf6b8c1cc43404126b514066ace8d8c71bcec2e99f759833ec84.svg) \n\nthen the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}= & [\\begin{aligned}-12 & 3 \\\\ 11 & 6\\end{aligned}]. \\\\  & \\,{↑}_{[{b}_{1}]_{C}}\\,{↑}_{[{b}_{2}]_{C}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe beauty of the change-of-coordinates matrix is that it allows us to express the vector $[\\mathbf x]_{\\mathcal B}$ in the basis $\\mathcal C$ via the transformation\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf x]_{\\mathcal C} = P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}} \\cdot [\\mathbf x]_{\\mathcal B}.\n$$\n\nThis can be visualized as shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ede6eb17f0667a8974902bf2d181150eeb3c665faa3059bb818bb9a61b631511.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125823 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have bases $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\mathcal{C}=\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\},$ and we want to find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C},$ denoted $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}}.$\n\nRecall that the columns of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}}$ are the coordinates of the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C},$ that is\n![](images/fb/fb63e6dcb2c7b0f8417e4f3fd394d42fa4bb84017552779903c40e4114639ac6.svg)\n\nTo compute $P_{\\small{\\color{black}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{black}\\mathcal{C}}},$ we need to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n![](images/64/644f78998195b8be26cb21bf5d680fbebed808d8f412769f9a3b06901533e87d.svg)\n\nIf we reduce the left-hand side of $M$ to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination, $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}}$ will emerge on the right-hand side:\n\n![](images/52/52f73b32e9700813960a9c544b71fedcce865ed3c452094be36615459663be9d.svg)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124721 :activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
